X-SOURCE-ID:ncwf
X-SOURCE:NC Wildlife Federation
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260921T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260921T160000
DTSTAMP:20260831T164653Z
CREATED:20260831T164652Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260831T164653Z
UID:10001771-1789995600-1790006400@ncwf.org
SUMMARY:Aho Branch Planting (Blowing Rock)
DESCRIPTION:Join NCWF High Country Wild and Blue Ridge Conservancy for a workday along Aho Branch at BRC’s Conservation Campus. The Campus\, located between Boone and Blowing Rock\, has become a community hub with a rain garden for stormwater runoff\, native landscape around the office\, half acre pollinator garden trail\, 2.2-mile public hiking trail\, shrub transition zone for wildlife habitat between the forest and field\, and an agricultural plot growing food for the community. \nDuring this workday\, we will direct our effort toward planting native plants and building deer proof cages to contribute to the larger ongoing stream and wetland restoration project on campus. This project will transform a former cattle grazing pasture into healthy aquatic and terrestrial habitat with riparian and vernal pool plantings along 800 linear feet of Aho Branch. Numerous wildlife species will benefit from this project including yellow-spotted salamanders and brook trout.  \nREGISTER HERE
URL:https://ncwf.org/event/aho-branch-planting-blowing-rock/
CATEGORIES:Featured
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://ncwf.org/wp-content/uploads/ahobranch.jpg
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar Group African American Cultural Center": "https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/before-we-knew-better-art-exhibition"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_group_african_american_cultural_center
X-SOURCE:NC State African American Cultural Center
DTSTAMP:20260905T041847Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53609784462079
DTSTART:20260921T140000Z
DTEND:20260921T210000Z
DESCRIPTION:The Before We Knew Better art exhibition brings together the wo
 rk of Lamar Whidbee and Chris Facey to reflect on joy\, memory and the exp
 eriences that shape us early in life. The exhibition looks to childhood as
  a place of imagination\, play and connection. Rather than seeing nostalgi
 a as a way to escape the present\, the exhibition considers how memories o
 f joy can remind us of what still matters. Whidbee and Facey ask what we c
 arry with us from those early years and how those experiences continue to 
 influence our well-being\, relationships and sense of self. At a time when
  conversations around mental health are increasingly important\, the exhib
 ition offers space to pause\, reflect and reconnect with the moments that 
 first taught us about joy. \n\nThe exhibition will be available for viewin
 g in the art gallery at the African American Cultural Center on weekdays f
 rom 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. between Aug. 17 and Sept. 25. Whidbee and Facey will
  discuss the exhibition during an artist talk event that will be held in t
 he center's art gallery at 6:30 p.m. Sept. 2.
GEO:35.785108;-78.674529
LOCATION:Witherspoon Student Center\, 218
SUMMARY:Before We Knew Better Art Exhibition
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/before-we-knew-better-art-exh
 ibition
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
