BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/applied-thematic-analysis-online"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260905T041913Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52152942831187
DTSTART:20260925T140000Z
DTEND:20260925T200000Z
DESCRIPTION:It’s Monday morning and you open your qualitative research pr
 oject folder. Thousands of pages of text\, distributed across 90 in-depth 
 interviews\, await. What’s next? This workshop provides a roadmap for co
 nducting an applied thematic analysis\, through all major steps in the ana
 lytic process. Participants will learn the benefits of writing a qualitati
 ve data analysis plan\, to ensure that the data collected are analyzed in 
 a way that best informs the research question(s) and fulfills the study’
 s objectives.\n\nThe instructors will demonstrate how to inductively ident
 ify themes\, transform those themes into codes\, and aggregate codes into 
 an evidence-based codebook that is easy to use and that will inform the st
 udy’s research question(s). Some time will be spent on the topic of inte
 grating codes and interpreting thematic structure. Basic data reduction te
 chniques will also be presented Hands-on exercises are included for those 
 who wish to practice the concepts learned.\n\nRegistration and fee (refund
 able for UNCCH students) are required for Zoom link access.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Applied Thematic Analysis (online)
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/applied-thematic-analysis-onli
 ne
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar Group African American Cultural Center": "https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/before-we-knew-better-art-exhibition"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_group_african_american_cultural_center
X-SOURCE:NC State African American Cultural Center
DTSTAMP:20260905T041847Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53391524823136
DTSTART:20260925T140000Z
DTEND:20260925T210000Z
DESCRIPTION:The Before We Knew Better art exhibition brings together the wo
 rk of Lamar Whidbee and Chris Facey to reflect on joy\, memory and the exp
 eriences that shape us early in life. The exhibition looks to childhood as
  a place of imagination\, play and connection. Rather than seeing nostalgi
 a as a way to escape the present\, the exhibition considers how memories o
 f joy can remind us of what still matters. Whidbee and Facey ask what we c
 arry with us from those early years and how those experiences continue to 
 influence our well-being\, relationships and sense of self. At a time when
  conversations around mental health are increasingly important\, the exhib
 ition offers space to pause\, reflect and reconnect with the moments that 
 first taught us about joy. \n\nThe exhibition will be available for viewin
 g in the art gallery at the African American Cultural Center on weekdays f
 rom 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. between Aug. 17 and Sept. 25. Whidbee and Facey will
  discuss the exhibition during an artist talk event that will be held in t
 he center's art gallery at 6:30 p.m. Sept. 2.
GEO:35.785108;-78.674529
LOCATION:Witherspoon Student Center\, 218
SUMMARY:Before We Knew Better Art Exhibition
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/before-we-knew-better-art-exh
 ibition
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events 1 Ics": "https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/choosing-sides-the-catawba-nation-the-fight-for-independence"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_1_ics
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
DTSTAMP:20260905T041836Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53312428104130
DTSTART:20260925T140000Z
DTEND:20260925T200000Z
DESCRIPTION:In partnership with the Catawba Cultural Center\, Kings Mountai
 n Historical Museum is excited to present\, "Choosing Sides: The Catawba N
 ation & the Fight for Independence." This exhibit explores the critical ro
 le of the Catawba Nation\, also known as Yį Iswąre or “People of the R
 iver\,” in the American Revolutionary War\, particularly in the backcoun
 try regions of Tryon and Lincoln Counties. As skilled warriors\, guides\, 
 and allies of the Patriot cause\, the Catawba people defended their homela
 nd\, protected settler communities\, and contributed to pivotal battles. D
 espite the hardships of disease\, displacement\, and warfare\, the Catawba
  demonstrated extraordinary resilience. Their story is one of courage\, al
 liance\, and survival\, woven into the struggle for independence in the So
 uthern Campaign of the Revolution.\n\nThe exhibit will run from June 30\, 
 2026 until October 24\, 2026. Admission is Free!
GEO:35.239004;-81.342181
LOCATION:Kings Mountain Historical Museum
SUMMARY:Choosing Sides: The Catawba Nation & The Fight for Independence
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/choosing-sides-the-catawba-n
 ation-the-fight-for-independence
CATEGORIES:Exhibits & Tours
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events 1 Ics": "https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/community-stories-sustaining-north-carolinas-black-heritage"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_1_ics
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
DTSTAMP:20260905T041836Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53138241967116
DTSTART:20260925T140000Z
DTEND:20260925T210000Z
DESCRIPTION:Community Stories: Sustaining North Carolina’s Black Heritage
  at the Gregg Museum of Art & Design presents a series of vignettes explor
 ing spaces of dignity\, belonging\, safety\, and imagination within Black 
 communities across North Carolina. Featuring historical photographs\, cont
 emporary images\, artworks from the Gregg Museum collection including a ha
 ndmade guitar by Freeman Vines\, and ephemera from community partners\, th
 e exhibition reflects on place\, memory\, creativity\, and spiritual resil
 ience across generations. Through stories of triumph and adversity\, Commu
 nity Stories honors the ways Black Americans have sustained and uplifted t
 heir communities through family\, entrepreneurship\, cultural expression\,
  and soulful imagination\, while inspiring future generations.
GEO:35.784925;-78.661662
LOCATION:Gregg Museum of Art & Design
SUMMARY:Community Stories: Sustaining North Carolina’s Black Heritage
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/community-stories-sustaining
 -north-carolinas-black-heritage
CATEGORIES:Exhibits & Tours
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
